fpcmoh: fix double free of the SSM error (crashes fprintd with SIGSEGV)

Both SSM completion callbacks overwrite their owned `error` parameter with a
borrowed pointer to the SSM's own error, then hand that borrowed pointer to a
(transfer full) function. The result is a leak plus a double free, and fprintd
dies with SIGSEGV on every failed enroll/verify/identify:

    fprintd.service: Main process exited, code=dumped, status=11/SEGV
    fprintd.service: Failed with result 'core-dump'.

libfprint gives the callback its own COPY of the error and keeps the original
(libfprint/fpi-ssm.c, fpi_ssm_mark_completed):

    if (machine->callback)
      {
        GError *error = machine->error ? g_error_copy (machine->error) : NULL;
        machine->callback (machine, machine->dev, error);
      }
    fpi_ssm_free (machine);

and fpi_ssm_free() then frees the original:

    g_clear_pointer (&machine->error, g_error_free);

while fpi_ssm_get_error() hands out a borrowed reference to that same original:

    return machine->error;

So in fpc_enroll_ssm_done()/fpc_verify_ssm_done():

    if (fpi_ssm_get_error (ssm))
      error = fpi_ssm_get_error (ssm);     /* leaks the copy we were given,   */
                                           /* and adopts machine->error       */
    fpi_device_enroll_complete (dev, NULL, error);   /* (transfer full): frees */
                                                     /* machine->error         */
    /* ... then fpi_ssm_free() frees machine->error again -> boom */

fpi_device_enroll_complete() and fpi_device_identify_complete() are both
annotated "@error: (transfer full)".

The `error` parameter already carries exactly the value these two lines were
trying to fetch, so the fix is simply to drop the override.

Observed on FPC 10a5:a900. The identify path had never reached this code with a
non-NULL error before -- it always failed earlier, in the FP_VERIFY_CANCEL
cleanup state (see patch 0002) -- which is why the crash only appeared once that
was fixed and an enroll got far enough to fail on its own.

This is not a900-specific and not related to that device's firmware quirks: any
FPC MoH device crashes fprintd whenever one of these state machines fails.

Applies on top of merge request 396.
---
--- a/libfprint/drivers/fpcmoh/fpc.c
+++ b/libfprint/drivers/fpcmoh/fpc.c
@@ -953,9 +953,10 @@

   fp_info ("Verify_identify complete!");

-  if (fpi_ssm_get_error (ssm))
-    error = fpi_ssm_get_error (ssm);
-
+  /* NB: do not overwrite `error` with fpi_ssm_get_error(). libfprint already
+   * passed us an owned copy of the SSM error; fpi_ssm_get_error() returns the
+   * SSM's original, which fpi_ssm_free() frees. Adopting it here would leak the
+   * copy and then double-free the original via the (transfer full) complete(). */
